<div>
    <div class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-orange-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Domain Events &amp; Locking</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            Say what happened in the domain and let other parts of the app react once the data is committed; run side effects only after a commit; refuse a save that would overwrite someone else&rsquo;s edit.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="domain-events" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Domain events</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        An event is a <a href="/docs/language/value-objects" class="text-cyan-300 hover:underline">value object</a> that inherits <code>DomainEvent</code>. A model records events on itself with <code>record_event</code>; they are published when the record is saved, so a method that raises an event and then fails to save publishes nothing. Elsewhere, <code>publish(event)</code> publishes at once.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre data-filename="app/models/order.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class OrderPaid &lt; DomainEvent
  number: String
  cents: Int
end

class Order &lt; Model
  def pay
    @status = "paid"
    @record_event(OrderPaid.new(@number, @cents))
  end
end

order.pay
order.save            # OrderPaid is published here, if the save succeeds</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="subscribers" class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Subscribers</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Subscriptions live in <code>app/subscribers/</code>, loaded after the models, services and jobs. A subscription to a class also receives its subclasses&rsquo; events.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre data-filename="app/subscribers/orders.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">subscribe(OrderPaid) do |event|
  LedgerEntry.create({"number": event.number, "cents": event.cents})
end

subscribe(OrderPaid, ReceiptJob)   # ReceiptJob.perform(args) gets event.to_h</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li>A <strong class="text-white">block</strong> handler runs in the same process once the data is committed: right away outside a transaction, after the outermost commit inside one, never if the transaction rolls back. A handler that throws fails the request, but the data is already committed &mdash; use a job for anything that can fail.</li>
        <li>A <strong class="text-white">job</strong> handler is enqueued when the event is published, with the event&rsquo;s fields as its arguments. Inside a transaction the job row is written by that transaction (<code>_jobs</code> lives on the default connection), so it commits or rolls back with the data &mdash; the <em>transactional outbox</em> &mdash; and the job queue retries delivery. On Cloudflare Workers jobs go to a Queue, which is not transactional.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="streams" class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Streaming to ES</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A stream subscription appends each event to a topic of an <a href="/docs/builtins/es" class="text-cyan-300 hover:underline">ES</a> broker (<code>ES_BROKER</code>), for other services to consume &mdash; keyed by the named field. It goes through the outbox: publishing enqueues the built-in <code>EventStreamJob</code> in the transaction, which appends the record once the data is committed, with the job queue&rsquo;s retries. A stream receives every committed event, at least once, and never a rolled-back one.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre data-filename="app/subscribers/streams.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">subscribe(OrderPaid, {"stream": "billing", "key": "number"})

# on the topic `billing`, key "A-1":
# {"type": "OrderPaid", "data": {"number": "A-1", "cents": 900}, "occurred_at": "2026-10-11T19:07:54Z"}</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="consuming" class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Consuming a stream</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        An application that reacts to another service&rsquo;s events reads the topic with a consumer group and publishes each record to its own subscribers. <code>EventStream.consume(topic, {"group": …, "partitions": 1, "max_records": 100})</code> rebuilds each record as its <code>DomainEvent</code> and publishes it &mdash; block and job subscribers run, stream subscriptions do not &mdash; committing the group&rsquo;s offset after each one (at least once). Records of an event class this application does not declare are skipped.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre data-filename="app/jobs/billing_events_job.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class BillingEventsJob
  static cron: String = Cron.every("1 minute")

  static def perform(args: Hash)
    EventStream.consume("billing", {"group": "shipping"})
  end
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="versioning" class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Versioning events</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        An event that changes shape declares <code>static version: Int = 2</code>; stream records carry the version they were written with. A consumer meeting an older record calls the class&rsquo;s <code>static def upcast(data, from_version)</code>, which returns the fields in the current shape. An older record without <code>upcast</code>, or one from a newer version than the class, stops <code>EventStream.consume</code> with an error before it is committed &mdash; nothing is dropped silently.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class InvoicePaid &lt; DomainEvent
  static version: Int = 2
  number: String
  cents: Int

  # v1 called the field `amount`.
  static def upcast(data: Hash, from_version: Int) -&gt; Hash
    return data unless from_version == 1

    {"number": data["number"], "cents": data["amount"]}
  end
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="after-commit" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">After the commit</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        <code>after_commit { … }</code> (or <code>after_commit do … end</code>) runs a block once the data is durable: after the outermost commit inside a transaction, never if it rolls back, at once outside one. It is the place for side effects that must not happen for data that was never stored.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">Order.transaction do
  order.pay
  order.save
  after_commit { broadcast("orders", {"paid": order.number}) }
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="optimistic-locking" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Optimistic locking</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Two people edit the same record: both load version 3, the first saves, and the second save would silently overwrite the first. Declare <code>optimistic_lock</code> and the second save is refused instead.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class Order &lt; Model
  optimistic_lock
end

order = Order.create({"number": "A-1"})   # lock_version: 0
a = Order.find(order._key)
b = Order.find(order._key)
a.number = "A-2"
a.save                  # true — lock_version is now 1
b.number = "A-3"
b.save                  # false
b.stale?                # true — _errors names lock_version</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Every update checks that the stored <code>lock_version</code> is still the one the record was read with, and bumps it, in one atomic step (a conditional <code>UPDATE</code> on the SQL adapters, an <code>If-Match</code> on the revision on SoliDB). The static <code>Model.update(key, changes)</code> checks the <code>lock_version</code> in <code>changes</code> when there is one &mdash; a form&rsquo;s hidden field &mdash; and the stored one otherwise. To answer HTTP, throw the built-in <code>StaleRecord</code>, a <code>DomainError</code> whose <code>http_status</code> is 409:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">throw StaleRecord.new("order #{order.number} changed since you opened it") unless order.save</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">On a column-mode table (an existing SQL schema, <code>table "orders"</code>) the table needs a <code>lock_version</code> integer column; the update is one <code>UPDATE … WHERE id = ? AND lock_version = ?</code>. <code>increment</code> and <code>decrement</code> do not bump the version.</p>

    <h2 id="aggregate-roots" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Aggregate roots</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        An aggregate is a root and the entities that only exist through it &mdash; an invoice and its lines. <code>part_of :invoice</code> on the child makes the root the only way in: its <code>create</code>, <code>save</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>upsert</code> and <code>create_many</code> are refused unless an <code>Invoice.transaction do … end</code> is running. Deleting an invoice that cascades to its lines goes inside that transaction as well; reads are not restricted.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">module Billing
  class Line &lt; Model
    part_of :invoice                # Billing::Invoice
  end
end

Billing::Line.create({...})         # error: part of the Billing::Invoice aggregate
Billing::Invoice.transaction do
  invoice.lines &lt;&lt; Billing::Line.new({"cents": 100})   # fine
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="bounded-contexts" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Bounded contexts</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Models can live in modules, one per part of the domain, each with its own names. A class nested in a module is <code>Billing::Invoice</code>, its collection <code>billing_invoices</code>, a module may be declared in several files, its classes are read unqualified inside it, and relations default to models of the same context (<code>class_name:</code> names any other).
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># app/models/billing/invoice.sl
module Billing
  class Invoice &lt; Model
    belongs_to("customer")          # Billing::Customer
  end
end

# app/models/shipping/invoice.sl
module Shipping
  class Invoice &lt; Model             # its own table: shipping_invoices
  end
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
